{
 "component": "QrPlaceholder",
 "version": 1,
 "summary": "A believable QR code picture for a ticket, a boarding pass or a pairing screen: a 21 × 21 module grid with the three 7 × 7 finder patterns and the timing lanes, data modules from a deterministic pseudo-random seed, black on white in every look inside a 4-module quiet zone, scaled to the square its box allows. It is a placeholder, not a scannable code.",
 "params": {
  "seed": {
   "type": "float",
   "default": 1,
   "doc": "Picks the module pattern: the same seed always draws the same code, another seed another one (a whole number; a \"@variable\" works too)"
  },
  "size": {
   "type": "float",
   "default": 160,
   "static": true,
   "doc": "Side in dp it asks the layout for; the drawing scales to the square the box really gets (min of width and height), centred"
  },
  "contentDescription": {
   "type": "string",
   "default": "QR code",
   "doc": "What a screen reader says: what the code is for, e.g. \"Boarding pass QR code for flight AF 1234\""
  },
  "ink": {
   "type": "color",
   "default": "#FF000000",
   "doc": "Module colour: black in every look, because scanners expect dark modules on a light ground. Change it only to another very dark colour"
  },
  "paper": {
   "type": "color",
   "default": "#FFFFFFFF",
   "doc": "Ground and quiet-zone colour: white in every look, dark and mono included"
  }
 },
 "capabilities": [
  "data.loop",
  "draw.shapes",
  "draw.transform",
  "a11y.labels"
 ],
 "call": {
  "QrPlaceholder": {
   "seed": 7,
   "contentDescription": "Boarding pass QR code"
  }
 },
 "pitfalls": [
  "Black on white in every look, on purpose: ink and paper default to literal #FF000000 and #FFFFFFFF, not theme colours, because a code inverted for dark or mono-dark would not scan (and would not look like one). In mono-dark that is a lit white square: keep it the size the task needs. Only the 1 dp frame round the quiet zone is a theme colour (outline), so the white square still has an edge on a white page",
  "Not a real QR code: there is no encoder in the format. The 441 modules come from two nested canvas loops (r, c), each module's paint alpha step((7c² + 13r² + 5cr + 3c + 11r + 17 seed) mod 23, 11), an integer hash every player computes exactly, so the pattern is the same in Java, C++ and TypeScript. For a scannable code, render it on the server and send a bitmap",
  "The finder corners and the row and column 6 timing lanes are cleared with paper and redrawn over the data, so they are always clean: 7 × 7 rings at three corners, alternating modules between them",
  "Drawn in a 29-module unit square (21 + 2 × 4 quiet zone) under one scale of min(width, height) / 29, centred: an 88 dp code in a 56 dp row is a 56 dp code, never cut. Modules overlap by 0.02 so no hairline shows between them at fractional scales, and the clearing rectangles reach 0.02 further so no sliver of a neighbour shows along them",
  "One accessibility node, the canvas, with contentDescription: say what the code is for"
 ],
